#
# This is an example keyboard config file for matchbox
#
# You can edit this file or change per user by copying to 
#  ~/.matchbox/kbdconfig
#
# Se the matchbox manual for more info ( http://projects.o-hand.com/matchbox )

### Window operation short cuts 

<Alt>n=next
<Alt>p=prev
<Alt>c=close
<Alt>d=desktop
<Alt>m=!matchbox-remote -mbmenu

### App launching.

<ctrl><alt>x=!xterm
<ctrl><alt>r=!rxvt

### App launching with startup notify

<ctrl><alt>e=!!gpe-calender

### windows style key shortcuts

<alt>Tab=next
<alt><shift>Tab=prev

<alt>space=taskmenu
<alt>escape=!matchbox-remote -mbmenu
<alt>f4=close

# f11=fullscreen
#
# Deliberately not bound on this device. The Sharp Zaurus thumb keyboard
# has no function-key row at all, so the *only* thing that can send F11
# here is the hardware OK button under the screen: the kernel board file
# reports it as CORGI_KEY_OK = KEY_F11 (arch/arm/mach-pxa/corgi.c), which
# evdev and XKB pass through unchanged as plain F11.
#
# A bare binding in this file becomes an XGrabKey() on the root window,
# which takes the key away from every client. That made the OK button a
# fullscreen toggle and, worse, meant matchbox-desktop never saw it -- so
# "select an icon with the arrows and press OK" did nothing. Leaving this
# commented is what lets the desktop bind OK as its activate key.
#
# <alt>f4=close above is fine to keep: Cancel sends bare F4, not <alt>F4.

### super should be a 'window' key, uncomment '#' if you have one.

#<super>d=desktop
#<super>r=!xterm
#<super>e=!!nautilus

### Special keys, likely probably only available on extended keyboards.

#App=!matchbox-remote -mbmenu
#Menu=!matchbox-remote -mbmenu

### Sharp Zaurus on/off button: NOT bound, on purpose.
#
# It is plain KEY_SUSPEND (row 7, col 0 -- CORGI_KEY_OFF in
# corgi_patched.c), like any other key here, so binding XF86Suspend was
# the obvious move -- but the project's zaurus XKB symbols file cannot
# actually give that keycode a keysym: adding ANY previously-undefined
# keycode there makes xsession's live xkbcomp upload to Xfbdev fail with
# a reproducible XKEYBOARD BadValue, independent of which keycode or
# keysym is used (tried three combinations). See
# docs/DEADLETTER-XKB-LIVE-SETMAP.md for the full investigation. Without
# a keysym on that keycode, XKeysymToKeycode has nothing to resolve
# XF86Suspend to, so a binding here would be silently inert -- this
# comment exists so the next person does not re-add it assuming it
# should just work.
#
#XF86Suspend=!/usr/sbin/suspend

### Sharp Zaurus Home button -> show the desktop.
#
# The four application buttons under the screen are matrix column 0 of
# corgikbd_keymap[] (corgi_patched.c): rows 3, 4 and 5 are
# CORGI_KEY_CALENDER/ADDRESS/MAIL, and row 6 -- the Home button, the one
# with the house on the keycap -- is plain KEY_SYSRQ. Nothing else on
# this keyboard reports KEY_SYSRQ, and nothing in this ROM used it, so
# grabbing it costs no other function.
#
# Unlike the on/off button above, this needs no change to the zaurus XKB
# symbols file and so does not run into the live-SetMap BadValue: the
# stock evdev keycodes already carry KEY_SYSRQ as <PRSC>, and pc's
# symbols already give <PRSC> the keysym Print at level 1. Print is
# therefore resolvable by XKeysymToKeycode() as-is.
#
# "showdesktop", not "desktop": the latter is a toggle, so a second Home
# press would drop back into the app you just left. Home should be
# one-way -- press it, you are on the desktop, press it again, you are
# still on the desktop. <Alt>d above keeps the toggle for anyone who
# wants it.
#
# The apps are not closed or iconified, only covered: matchbox shows one
# app at a time anyway, and the desktop is just another client being
# raised above them. mb-applet-menu-launcher and the task menu
# (<Alt>space) still list every running app afterwards.
Print=showdesktop

